#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "M1")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U1 Finite fields",[List the subfield sizes of $FF_(2^8)$ and justify that the list is complete.],[Use divisors of eight.],[The allowed degrees are one, two, four, and eight.],[The sizes are $2,4,16,256$, one subfield of each size. Necessity follows from the tower law and sufficiency and uniqueness from the finite-field subfield theorem.],[State all three aspects: necessity, existence, uniqueness.]),
 p(2,"E","U5 Tensor computation",[Compute $ZZ/15ZZ otimes_ZZ ZZ/21ZZ$.],[Use the sum of the two annihilator ideals.],[Their gcd is three.],[The tensor is $ZZ/(15ZZ+21ZZ)=ZZ/3ZZ$, by the cyclic tensor formula and Bézout.],[Name the canonical generator tensor.]),
 p(3,"C","U3–U4 Presentations",[Identify the module generated by $a,b$ with relations $a=3b$ and $10b=0$, and decide whether it is projective over $ZZ$.],[Eliminate $a$.],[A section of $ZZ -> ZZ/10ZZ$ cannot exist.],[The module is $ZZ/10ZZ$ via $b|->[1]$, $a|->[3]$, with the inverse sending $[1]$ to $b$. It is not projective: lifting its identity across the quotient map would give a section, but the image of $[1]$ would be an integer killed by ten and hence zero, contradicting the section equation.],[Give the isomorphism and a separate projectivity argument.]),
 p(4,"C","U6 Localization",[Compute $ZZ[1/3]otimes_ZZ ZZ/12ZZ$.],[Quotient the localized ring by twelve.],[Three is a unit, so the ideal generated by twelve equals that generated by four.],[The tensor is $ZZ[1/3]/12ZZ[1/3]=ZZ[1/3]/4ZZ[1/3]$. Since three is already invertible modulo four, this quotient is $ZZ/4ZZ$. The natural reduction map sends $a/3^n$ to $[a][3]^(-n)$.],[Explain the last localization identification.]),
 p(5,"A","U2–U3 Simple quotients",[Prove every nonzero finitely generated module over a commutative unital ring has a maximal proper submodule.],[Apply Zorn to proper submodules.],[Finite generation prevents a chain union from becoming the whole module without one member doing so.],[The poset of proper submodules is nonempty. For a nonempty chain, its union is a submodule; the empty chain has the proper upper bound zero because $M$ is nonzero. If the union were all of $M$, finitely many generators would all lie in one member, forcing that member to be $M$, a contradiction. Thus every chain has a proper upper bound. Zorn gives a maximal proper submodule, whose quotient is simple.],[Use finite generation exactly in the properness check.]),
 p(6,"A","U7–U8 Operators",[Let $T^3=1$ over $CC$. Prove the space is a direct sum of the three eigenspaces and describe projections using interpolation.],[The roots of $t^3-1$ are distinct.],[Use polynomials equal to one at one root and zero at the others.],[For roots $lambda_i$, set $e_i(t)=product_(j!=i)(t-lambda_j)/(lambda_i-lambda_j)$. Modulo $t^3-1$, these are orthogonal idempotents summing to one and satisfy $(t-lambda_i)e_i=0$. Substituting $T$ gives projections onto the corresponding eigenspaces, with sum the identity and pairwise zero products. Their images therefore give the direct-sum decomposition.],[Explain why polynomial congruences remain true after substituting $T$.]),
)
